<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nieuwe Waterweg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as New Waterway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nieuwe Waterweg is a ship canal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, opened in 1872, that links <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at Hook of Holland. Designed by the engineer Pieter Caland to replace silting river channels, it gave large ships direct access to Rotterdam and was the basis of the city's growth into one of the world's largest ports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nieuwe-waterweg.jpg" alt="Nieuwe Waterweg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DeltaWorks Online - Eszter Simonfi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maeslantkering.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
